  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 302 AM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nToday...Regional IR satellite imagery shows convection ongoing \nacross the Dakotas and northern MN early this morning. Some \nshowers may continue into western or central MN, though upper \nlevel forcing will taper off and allow most places to remain dry\nuntil later today.\n\nAs the mid-level shortwave amplifies overhead this afternoon,  \nsurface cyclogenesis off the lee of the Rockies will be the\ntrigger for scattered convection over a relatively broad area \nacross the central and northern Plains. A couple thousand Joules\nof MUCAPE and about 35 kts of bulk wind shear may support \nisolated supercells or mature line segments capabale of severe \nwinds. This would primarily be during the afternoon and evening \nwith the initial round of storms across the Dakotas and parts of\nwestern MN.\n\nThrough the evening, storms will likely become more stratiform\nand overspread across a larger area as the main lobe of 500mb \nvorticity moves through SW MN. Low-level winds also increase \novernight, wrapping moisture around the low pressure center. At \nthe same time, you have some sense of a TROWAL/fgen forcing on \nthe northern edge of the low, almost similar to what we see with\na number of our strong winter low pressure systems. \"Imagine if\nthis was all snow\" is fair to bring up, but we'll save that for\nanother day as we have more important business to attend to. \nForecasting QPF is often a very humbling thing, especially in \nthese convective scenarios. Not everyone is going to see 4+ \ninches of rain, but there certainly is a good signal that some \nareas will. Both physical and computer based guidance have \nfocused in the MN River Valley (roughly from Granite Falls to \nMankato) as the likely corridor for pockets of the heaviest \nrainfall amounts. This is the area where the deepest moisture \noverlaps with the greatest vorticity aloft. A Flood Watch was \ncontemplated, however, given 1) the very dry antecedent \nconditions, 2) the slow-moving nature of this system (rain falls\nover a longer period rather than training storms or a very \nintense MCS), and 3) the greater capacity of mature farmlands \nout in those areas, we felt that any flooding will likely be \nlocalized to any fast responding creeks and streams or low- \nlying areas. That said, always remember to Turn Around, Don't\nDrown when encountering any flooded roadways.\n\nThe amount of rainfall outside of this corridor is more\nuncertain and largely dependent on individual storm tracks or \npersistence of the stratiform rain Friday morning. Also not \ndissimilar to winter systems, we will likely have some influence\nfrom drier air with winds turning out of the northeast along and\nnorth of the I-94 corridor (including St Cloud and the Twin \nCities metro into western WI). Although in this case, the \"drier\nair\" still has a pool of 1\" PWATS. It will take a lot of bad \nluck for these areas to not see ANY rain, though history is \ncertainly not on our side for the way this summer has gone. Both\nSTC and MSP airports are staggeringly in the red for \nprecipitation through the year so far. As of yesterday, STC's \nprecip departure from normal (1991-2020) is -3.72\", and MSP's \nis -4.35\". EAU is doing a little better, but still 1.00\" behind\nnormal as well.\n\nSomewhat of an interesting evolution with the recent CAMS is\nthat we may see additional storm development along the frontal\nboundary of the dry and moist airmasses Friday \nafternoon/evening. This may provide a better chance for rain in \nthe areas that miss out on the heaviest amounts Thursday night \nand Friday morning, especially in a solution similar to the 7/29\n18z run of the RRFS.\n\nThis Weekend...Saturday and Sunday continue to look like great\ndays with mostly sunny skies and temperatures in the low to \nmid 80s each afternoon.\n\nNext Week...Winds shift out of the south as a strong low\npressure system develops across northern Saskatchewan and \nManitoba. A cold front will likely drape across the northern \nPlains and spark a round of storms Monday into Tuesday. A \ncorridor of moist and unstable air will develop out ahead of the\nfront, and if paired with enough vertical shear, could produce \na zone for organized convection. The timing may end up being a \nlimiting factor for any severe weather in the Upper MS Valley, \nhowever, as the front is currently favored to end up moving \nthrough overnight while instability decreases. Storm chances \nmay persist into Tuesday afternoon, but invading high pressure \nwill result in quieter weather for the middle of the week.",
